  "last_executor_claim": "Report written to `/app/report.md`. Key findings:\n\n- **Effectiveness:** Four-stream mandatory sorting is precedented (China's national model uses the same four categories). Outcomes vary hugely — recycling rates range ~1% to ~61% depending on enforcement, access, and infrastructure. Access ≠ participation: ~73% of U.S. households have recycling access but only ~43% participate.\n- **Organics:** Green-bin waste typically goes to composting (dominant; California needs 75–100 new facilities for its mandate) or anaerobic digestion → biogas/RNG. Contamination is the top barrier — only ~4% of U.S. food waste reaches composters despite 49% growth in curbside organics collection since 2021.\n- **Route optimization:** The standard approach is CVRP/mixed-integer models plus heuristic/IoT-sensor methods; published results show 14–30%+ reductions in collection distance. Your city's paired-stream pickup days (green+grey, green+blue) match the dedicated-vehicle multi-stream model.\n- **Common failure modes:** free-riding and low voluntary participation, contamination/wishcycling (~20% typical U.S. curbside audit rate), the intention–behavior gap, missing downstream processing capacity, volatile recycling markets, weak enforcement credibility, and complexity burden — including infrequent hazardous collection, which risks storage/dumping.\n\nBiggest caveat in the report: Neo-Veridia's ordinance specifies no downstream organics processor, which the literature identifies as the decisive variable; the corpus also lacks a direct quantitative evaluation of an identical four-bin scheme.",
